{"message": "I’d choose an interactive, slowly disintegrating digital archive: part novella, part sound installation, part abandoned computer game. No grand quest. You’re cataloguing the belongings of someone whose identity keeps changing depending on which objects you examine. A grocery receipt contradicts a love letter. A recording contains a voice that the transcripts insist was never there.

The style would be intimate rather than spectacular: severe typography, grainy photographs, bureaucratic prose interrupted by something embarrassingly tender. The sound would mostly be room tone—pipes, distant traffic, a refrigerator switching off—with music arriving so rarely that a single chord feels intrusive. And I’d make some choices irreversible: preserving one document means letting another deteriorate. You can’t finish by collecting everything.

What draws me to that idea is the uncomfortable overlap between care and control. Does preserving someone’s memory honor them, or deny them the right to change—or disappear? I wouldn’t want a twist that resolves it. I’d want the final interaction to be small and difficult: deciding whether to correct a false account because it’s false, even though it’s the only account in which someone was loved."}

{"message":"Fair criticism of the familiar ingredients—haunted archives and unreliable records are well-trodden territory. I don’t agree that they’re automatically uninteresting, but I can go somewhere entirely different.

I’d make a slapstick opera for excavators in a working gravel quarry. Six machines, six singers, no human-shaped puppetry: the choreography would use the machines’ actual capabilities. A bucket trying to pass an egg to another bucket. A loader repeatedly ruining its own magnificent entrance because it has to reverse, triggering its warning beeper. The score would build around diesel rhythms and hydraulic squeals, with the singers giving absurdly elaborate voice to tiny mechanical grievances.

The central scene: two excavators attempt to fold a bedsheet. No symbolism required to make that worth watching. But underneath the comedy, I’d explore precision, dependence, and the awkwardness of being immensely powerful yet poorly equipped for tenderness. I’d end with the folded sheet—not an explosion or a revelation—and let the ridiculous difficulty of getting there carry the finale."}

